    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.06-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A crude death rate can rise as a population ages despite improvements in age-specific survival. Such an increase does not automatically mean a return to stage one's conditions. Age composition matters. The basic model is useful for broad contrasts but should not replace indicators showing how survival differs at particular ages. This prevents a single crude rate from becoming an unsupported judgement of healthcare quality.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-3.1.06-1",
        "CL-6137-3.1.06-2",
